" The", "lp": -0.4866792857646942, "top": [[" The", -0.4866792857646942], [" `", -1.6085549592971802]]}, {"t": " `", "lp": -0.3332991898059845, "top": [[" `", -0.3332991898059845], [" driver", -2.4516005516052246]]}, {"t": "xe", "lp": -0.728024959564209, "top": [["xe", -0.728024959564209], ["cc", -0.769172191619873]]}, {"t": "`", "lp": -0.1231154352426529, "top": [["`", -0.1231154352426529], [".k", -2.606445074081421]]}, {"t": " driver", "lp": -0.14719019830226898, "top": [[" driver", -0.14719019830226898], [" module", -2.109952926635742]]}, {"t": " module", "lp": -0.7097758054733276, "top": [[" module", -0.7097758054733276], [" parameter", -2.060565710067749]]}, {"t": " parameters", "lp": -0.36877620220184326, "top": [[" parameters", -0.36877620220184326], [" parameter", -1.4928282499313354]]}, {"t": " documentation", "lp": -0.6152488589286804, "top": [[" documentation", -0.6152488589286804], [" in", -1.1999541521072388]]}, {"t": "\n", "lp": -0.018159667029976845, "top": [["\n", -0.018159667029976845], [" in", -5.267921447753906]]}, {"t": "-", "lp": -0.00048768488341011107, "top": [["-", -0.00048768488341011107], ["-community", -9.62647533416748]]}, {"t": " Intel", "lp": -0.7268370985984802, "top": [[" Intel", -0.7268370985984802], [" Recent", -1.2779464721679688]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing / preemption-based**\n- CCS contexts are allocated dynamically from the available CCS instances\n- The driver manages context migration between GCS and CCS\n- More flexible but can have overhead from context management\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Static/dedicated CCS assignment**\n- CCS contexts are pinned to specific CCS instances\n- More predictable behavior since contexts don't migrate\n- Lower overhead but less flexible allocation\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **Disabled / no CCS usage**\n- All compute work runs on GCS (General Compute Slices)\n- CCS instances are not used for context management\n\n## When to use each\n\n**Mode 1** is generally the default and good for most workloads\u2014it offers the best balance of flexibility and performance.\n\n**Mode 2** is useful when you want deterministic behavior and want to avoid the overhead of context migration, particularly in scenarios where predictable latency matters.\n\n**Mode 4** would be used if:\n- You're debugging driver issues related to CCS handling\n- You encounter instability or crashes specifically tied to CCS contexts\n- You want to isolate whether CCS is the source of a problem\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I want to be transparent that my knowledge of the exact semantics of these specific `ccs_mode` values may not be fully accurate. The Xe driver's CCS mode parameterization is fairly specialized, and I could be conflating details.\n\nFor precise, authoritative information, I'd recommend checking:\n- The kernel source documentation (`drivers/gpu/driver/xe/`)\n- The `xe` driver module parameters documentation\n- Intel"}